    <li class="nav-item" role="presentation"><button class="nav-link" id="tab-billing" data-bs-toggle="tab" data-bs-target="#pane-billing" type="button" role="tab" aria-controls="pane-billing" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Billing</button></li>
  </ul>

So after ArrowRight press, the active class remained on Notifications (which is disabled). So the keydown handler didn't skip disabled tab.

Looking at the tab.js code: In _keydown method, they check if key is arrow left/right/up/down, then they prevent default and stop propagation, compute isNext based on ArrowRight/Down, then get next active element via getNextActiveElement(this._getChildren(), event.target, isNext, true). Then they call Tab.getOrCreateInstance(nextActiveElement).show().

Thus the issue likely lies in getNextActiveElement function (in util/index.js) where the fourth argument is isCycleAllowed (true). It should skip disabled elements? Let's examine getNextActiveElement.

From util/index.js:

```javascript
const getNextActiveElement = (list, activeElement, shouldGetNext, isCycleAllowed) => {
  const listLength = list.length
  let index = list.indexOf(activeElement)

  // if the element does not exist in the list return an element
  // depending on the direction and if cycle is allowed
  if (index === -1) {
    return !shouldGetNext && isCycleAllowed ? list[listLength - 1] : list[0]
  }

  index += shouldGetNext ? 1 : -1

  if (isCycleAllowed) {
    index = (index + listLength) % listLength
  }

  return list[Math.max(0, Math.min(index, listLength - 1))]
}
```

This function does not consider disabled state; it just returns the element at the next index, regardless of disabled attribute. So if the next element is disabled, it will still be returned.

In the tab component, they want to skip disabled tabs. The logic should find the next enabled element (not disabled). Possibly they need to filter out disabled elements in the list before calling getNextActiveElement, or modify getNextActiveElement to respect disabled.

But the fix should be minimal and localized. Since the bug is in tab.js's _keydown method, we could adjust the logic to find the next enabled element by looping until we find an enabled one, using getNextActiveElement repeatedly, or we could filter the children list to exclude disabled ones before passing to getNextActiveElement.

